---
import IdeShowcase from './IdeShowcase.tsx';

// Comment style in this file: JSX-expression comments, not HTML comments —
// Astro passes HTML comments straight through to the page, where visitors read
// them in view-source.
//
// Every figure below is checked against the code, because a credibility strip
// that inflates its own numbers is worse than no strip: 25 is the count of
// Unity-specific agent tools in `unity-tools/index.ts`, 31 is the analyzer rule
// count in `unity-analyzers/rules/`, and 2021.3 is what the Unity package
// declares it supports.
const proof = [
  ['25', 'Unity-specific AI tools'],
  ['31', 'Unity analyzers'],
  ['6', 'formats edited natively'],
  ['2021.3+', 'through Unity 6'],
];
---

{/* Padding is the hero's real subject.

    Measured at a 1440x900 viewport, the previous values put the top of the
    product shot at y=543 and the shot is 482px tall — so the thing the whole
    page is built around was clipped at 74%, its status bar never seen, and
    roughly 60% of the first screen went to five stacked lines of text. The
    window did not need to be redesigned. It needed to be ON SCREEN.

    The saving comes from the gaps BETWEEN things, not from the space above the
    headline. A first pass cut this to pt-12 and put the h1's cap line at y=64 —
    exactly where the 64px fixed navbar ends, so the headline had no air at all
    and the page opened feeling cramped. 96px is the floor here: it clears the
    navbar by a comfortable 32px at every breakpoint. */}
<section class="relative overflow-hidden border-b border-border bg-void pb-16 pt-24 sm:pb-20">
  {/* Luminance only, no hue: the ground falls away from the product plane,
      which is the app's own recessed-chrome rule rather than a gradient wash.
      Re-aimed at the window rather than at the headline, and reaching further
      down the section, so the light is on the product instead of on the copy. */}
  <div
    class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 top-0 h-[1000px]"
    style="background:radial-gradient(ellipse 68% 52% at 50% 56%, hsl(250 19% 13% / 0.95) 0%, transparent 74%)"
  >
  </div>

  <div class="container relative mx-auto px-6">
    {/* Centered, deliberately. The copy's job here is to frame a product shot,
        and symmetry is what gives the window the stage. Earlier revisions ran
        this block left-aligned against a narrow two-pane panel, which read
        editorial when it needed to read like a launch. */}
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-[54rem] text-center">
      {/* By Unity devs, for Unity devs — and the copy has to SOUND like that
          rather than announce it. "We wanted" and "cost us a day" are the whole
          device: first person, a specific grievance, no marketing verb. A line
          that claimed the credential outright ("built by Unity developers") is
          the one thing that would break it, because anyone can write that
          sentence and only someone who shipped in this engine writes these.

          "The IDE Unity never built" was an earlier draft and read as a swipe at
          the engine. This is the same claim from the inside. */}
      <h1 class="font-display text-step3 font-semibold leading-[1.04] text-foreground sm:text-step4">
        We wanted a better IDE for Unity. So we built one.
      </h1>

      {/* Still a list, because completeness is the pitch — but the sentence
          before it says WHY the list is that long, which is the part that only
          someone who has shipped in this engine would write. */}
      <p class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-[72ch] text-step1 leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
        Every panel here exists because something in Unity cost us a day. Hierarchy,
        ScriptableObjects, UI canvas, input maps, console, tests and a debugger —
        with an AI wired into the running Editor.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-center gap-x-8 gap-y-4">
        <a
          href="#download"
          class="inline-flex h-12 items-center justify-center rounded-panel bg-primary px-8 text-base font-semibold text-primary-foreground transition-colors duration-150 hover:bg-primary-lit"
        >
          Download for free
        </a>
        <a
          href="/docs/"
          class="text-base font-medium text-foreground/80 underline decoration-border decoration-1 underline-offset-[7px] transition-colors duration-150 hover:text-foreground hover:decoration-primary"
        >
          Read the docs
        </a>
      </div>

      {/* This line is doing competitive work, so it has to be exact.

          "Free plan." said nothing a reader could act on — every tool has a free
          plan, and the one this product is measured against has one too. What
          that one does NOT have is commercial use: Rider's free tier is
          non-commercial only, which `STANDOUT-FEATURES.md` names as the gap that
          excludes the entire studio audience.

          The line answers the FEAR rather than stating the terms. What a Unity
          dev evaluating an editor actually worries about is not today's price —
          it is the invoice that arrives the day the project starts earning.
          "Not now, not when you ship" names that moment and closes it, which no
          restatement of the licence ("commercial projects included", an earlier
          draft) manages, because a reader who has been caught once reads terms
          looking for the clause that will catch them again.

          Two sentences, deliberately. The second is the one doing the work, and
          a comma would have let the eye run past it.

          Platforms are deliberately not here. They were the second half of this
          line and they are trivia at this height — the download table names all
          three targets with their versions, and the meta description carries
          them for search. Cutting them lets the sentence be one claim.

          Verified before writing: the free tier carries no use restriction
          anywhere in the repo, and the Unity package ships MIT. */}
      {/* Promoted from 13px muted to 16px foreground. The mark is drawn by
          `.claim-mark` in global.css; the size and colour are what make it hold
          attention once the motion has finished, since an animation nobody is
          looking at when it fires has to leave something behind. */}
      <p class="mt-5 text-base font-medium text-foreground">
        <span class="claim-mark">No licence to buy. Not now, not when you ship.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* The window is the hero. Everything above is its caption. */}
  <div class="relative mx-auto mt-9 w-full max-w-[1560px] px-6 sm:mt-11">
    <IdeShowcase client:load />

    {/* The proof strip sits under the window rather than above it, so it reads
        as a caption on something you have just seen rather than as a claim you
        are asked to take on faith. */}
    <dl
      class="mx-auto mt-10 grid max-w-4xl grid-cols-2 gap-x-6 gap-y-6 border-t border-border pt-8 sm:grid-cols-4"
    >
      {proof.map(([figure, label]) => (
        <div class="text-center">
          <dt class="font-mono text-step1 font-medium tabular-nums text-primary">{figure}</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-micro leading-snug text-muted-foreground">{label}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>
